> To capture the impacts of sea-level rise (SLR) in our model while avoiding double-counting of discontinuities from the accelerated decline of the Greenland and West Antarctic ice sheets, we adopt the approach from Yumashev et al. (2021). The governing equation for SLR is represented as follows:
> 
> \[
> \frac{d \text{SLR}}{dt} = \frac{\text{SLR}_{\text{eq}}(T) - \text{SLR}}{\tau_{\text{SLR}}}
> \]
> 
> where \(\text{SLR}_{\text{eq}}(T) = \text{SENS}_{\text{SLR}} \cdot T + \text{SLR}_{\infty}\) represents the equilibrium SLR as a linear function of global mean surface temperature (GMST) anomaly. Here, \(\text{SENS}_{\text{SLR}}\) denotes sensitivity of SLR to GMST changes, and \(\text{SLR}_{\infty}\) is the asymptotic value reflecting pre-industrial conditions.
> 
> The time constant \(\tau_{\text{SLR}}\) is modeled with a Gamma distribution to align with percentile estimates of global SLR in 2100 under SSP5 from Nauels et al. (2017), with additional Antarctic Ice Sheet discharge considerations. Percentiles from this source were used to optimize \(\tau_{\text{SLR}}\) parameters via the @Risk Optimiser, achieving a mode of 362 years and a mean of 386 years. In canonical Gamma distribution form, this corresponds to a shape parameter \(k = \frac{\text{Mean}}{\text{Mean} - \text{Mode}} = 16\) and a scale parameter \(\theta = \text{Mean} - \text{Mode} = 24\) years.
> 
> The resultant distribution provides a fat-tailed decay for the relative rate of sea level rise \(\lambda_{\text{SLR}} = \frac{1}{\tau_{\text{SLR}}}\) with a power law decay of \(-(1 + k) = -17\). Our model derived a Monte Carlo mean SLR of 1.57 meters by 2100 under the RCP8.5 scenario, about 50% higher than IPCC AR5 estimates, incorporating recent findings on potential rapid ice sheet collapse (Golledge et al., 2015; Hansen et al., 2016; Le Bars et al., 2017).
> 
> This calibration approach is detailed in Supplementary Table 11 of Yumashev et al. (2021).
